[{:db/id 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-048f2885-159c-5d24-83a6-9ee301309f6c" "question-310c6308-d59f-5c23-bf3f-94f16264b626"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129319 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1a5498dd-6e1c-5317-8d48-505ce94b33dc" "question-969f9c5c-90f0-5451-a64e-6e16b1103b2e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129320 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b3bc80a6-ca15-5095-bc92-205f586735f6" "question-d124471f-78aa-50f9-bfd3-0eadf2ea5bb7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-048f2885-159c-5d24-83a6-9ee301309f6c" :question/id #uuid "048f2885-159c-5d24-83a6-9ee301309f6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-139070" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{3},\\,y={2}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({2}^{t})={2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{({2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{2}^{2t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5dd24f4-4688-5465-a47d-3059c114e3fd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5dd24f4-4688-5465-a47d-3059c114e3fd" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5dd24f4-4688-5465-a47d-3059c114e3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" "answer-53eb5f8d-2c90-56b2-af3c-af00df5e8365" "answer-aa6427b7-b0e6-5e33-9aca-612cfab836af" "answer-97765c6f-bd17-5d3b-a5c6-230f3b86329e" "answer-217edb44-0fc7-5d6d-964e-6c05dca7cb50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" :answer/id #uuid "c63274df-6f1f-56f7-bcf0-d35e3a9b440e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+{4}^{t}{ln}^{2}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53eb5f8d-2c90-56b2-af3c-af00df5e8365" :answer/id #uuid "53eb5f8d-2c90-56b2-af3c-af00df5e8365" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa6427b7-b0e6-5e33-9aca-612cfab836af" :answer/id #uuid "aa6427b7-b0e6-5e33-9aca-612cfab836af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{3t}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97765c6f-bd17-5d3b-a5c6-230f3b86329e" :answer/id #uuid "97765c6f-bd17-5d3b-a5c6-230f3b86329e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-217edb44-0fc7-5d6d-964e-6c05dca7cb50" :answer/id #uuid "217edb44-0fc7-5d6d-964e-6c05dca7cb50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{t}\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+{2}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-310c6308-d59f-5c23-bf3f-94f16264b626" :question/id #uuid "310c6308-d59f-5c23-bf3f-94f16264b626" :question/math-academy-id "q-139066" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2t,\\,y=3t+2,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+{(3)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\sqrt{13}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ebb6a09-3962-51d8-a359-86c686b5cd13"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ebb6a09-3962-51d8-a359-86c686b5cd13" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebb6a09-3962-51d8-a359-86c686b5cd13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1889d211-580a-525d-8863-d0bc666144a5" "answer-d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" "answer-c1dbc8d7-24ce-50f6-8164-8b4e61b2fccd" "answer-fb1ce31b-4cfb-5eab-8d4f-6c08ab46d8c0" "answer-d33dff8a-de5a-5820-bbc2-521bfdade558"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1889d211-580a-525d-8863-d0bc666144a5" :answer/id #uuid "1889d211-580a-525d-8863-d0bc666144a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(5t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" :answer/id #uuid "d855760e-bbf6-5e09-b377-fb92b66bdfbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1dbc8d7-24ce-50f6-8164-8b4e61b2fccd" :answer/id #uuid "c1dbc8d7-24ce-50f6-8164-8b4e61b2fccd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{13}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb1ce31b-4cfb-5eab-8d4f-6c08ab46d8c0" :answer/id #uuid "fb1ce31b-4cfb-5eab-8d4f-6c08ab46d8c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}2t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d33dff8a-de5a-5820-bbc2-521bfdade558" :answer/id #uuid "d33dff8a-de5a-5820-bbc2-521bfdade558" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t(3t+2)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1a5498dd-6e1c-5317-8d48-505ce94b33dc" :question/id #uuid "1a5498dd-6e1c-5317-8d48-505ce94b33dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-139054" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={t}^{2},\\,y=t,\\,t\\in [0,2].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=2.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61ae82f2-6c7c-57cb-9b66-5fd5901e4df5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61ae82f2-6c7c-57cb-9b66-5fd5901e4df5" :answer-field/id #uuid "61ae82f2-6c7c-57cb-9b66-5fd5901e4df5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4e77fce-c9b3-50a6-aca4-e69c1a78f228" "answer-1c721d8c-9e46-58a3-95bb-3174862c4a01" "answer-df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" "answer-dcc1610f-6e04-5f46-8bfb-9596dcf2e267" "answer-5f781be2-8bd2-520a-9a09-5f6c042226c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4e77fce-c9b3-50a6-aca4-e69c1a78f228" :answer/id #uuid "e4e77fce-c9b3-50a6-aca4-e69c1a78f228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c721d8c-9e46-58a3-95bb-3174862c4a01" :answer/id #uuid "1c721d8c-9e46-58a3-95bb-3174862c4a01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" :answer/id #uuid "df954cef-dd13-5a8f-ac48-dd0715a02bf0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc1610f-6e04-5f46-8bfb-9596dcf2e267" :answer/id #uuid "dcc1610f-6e04-5f46-8bfb-9596dcf2e267" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f781be2-8bd2-520a-9a09-5f6c042226c2" :answer/id #uuid "5f781be2-8bd2-520a-9a09-5f6c042226c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-969f9c5c-90f0-5451-a64e-6e16b1103b2e" :question/id #uuid "969f9c5c-90f0-5451-a64e-6e16b1103b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-139055" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=t+1,\\,y=2{t}^{3},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(6{t}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ffdc95ce-c8de-554f-af86-018019927d83"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ffdc95ce-c8de-554f-af86-018019927d83" :answer-field/id #uuid "ffdc95ce-c8de-554f-af86-018019927d83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-95766dce-20ec-56a7-bf74-a65bab0ccb22" "answer-4b3a2be8-e4d5-5828-9be5-65cc4722dd19" "answer-579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" "answer-29c95824-df17-58a2-8aac-76df26add78b" "answer-25838e3e-57a1-5fd9-8feb-f77678c5ccc6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95766dce-20ec-56a7-bf74-a65bab0ccb22" :answer/id #uuid "95766dce-20ec-56a7-bf74-a65bab0ccb22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1){t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b3a2be8-e4d5-5828-9be5-65cc4722dd19" :answer/id #uuid "4b3a2be8-e4d5-5828-9be5-65cc4722dd19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" :answer/id #uuid "579fa76c-1f64-56b5-b97c-4581e9fe53fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(t+1)\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29c95824-df17-58a2-8aac-76df26add78b" :answer/id #uuid "29c95824-df17-58a2-8aac-76df26add78b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{1+36{t}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25838e3e-57a1-5fd9-8feb-f77678c5ccc6" :answer/id #uuid "25838e3e-57a1-5fd9-8feb-f77678c5ccc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}t(t+1)\\sqrt{1+6{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b3bc80a6-ca15-5095-bc92-205f586735f6" :question/id #uuid "b3bc80a6-ca15-5095-bc92-205f586735f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-352092" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{t}{4},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)].\n$$\n\nDetermine the surface area $S$ generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[{e}^{t}]={e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{t}{4}]={e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{[{e}^{t}]}^{2}+{[{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{4}]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{16}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{4t}+\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{16}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recognizing that the term under the square root is a perfect square, we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}({e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u={e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$  We also update the limits of integration for $u:$\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{e}^{t}({e}^{2t}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}({u}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\pi {[\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{u}{4}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{2}{4})-(\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}] \\\\  & =2\\pi [\\frac{28}{12}+\\frac{3}{12}] \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot \\frac{31}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{31\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-019c2510-13c6-5cb5-adc7-f6823183b11a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-019c2510-13c6-5cb5-adc7-f6823183b11a" :answer-field/id #uuid "019c2510-13c6-5cb5-adc7-f6823183b11a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128" :answer-field/choices ["answer-717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128" :answer/id #uuid "717f0fc3-0cb4-5e82-a464-045bfd255128" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{31\\pi}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d124471f-78aa-50f9-bfd3-0eadf2ea5bb7" :question/id #uuid "d124471f-78aa-50f9-bfd3-0eadf2ea5bb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-138972" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,y=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity $\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2}={sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}),$ we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\sqrt{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}).$ Then, $\\text{d}u=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\pi $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(1-{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{0}-2(1-{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{u}^{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =16\\pi \\cdot {[u-\\frac{{u}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =16\\pi (1-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =16\\pi (\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f67f1dc2-47fa-5fa4-a382-f69736f1f021"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f67f1dc2-47fa-5fa4-a382-f69736f1f021" :answer-field/id #uuid "f67f1dc2-47fa-5fa4-a382-f69736f1f021" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0c3ac195-5a28-5c11-99ed-a06a6384cb26" "answer-333420d8-3841-5986-a880-12239e3b0c86" "answer-8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" "answer-867968e5-1470-5d6b-bbb2-c22bb39822ef" "answer-206381c1-a55a-5bbf-8129-7ade3cea9753"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c3ac195-5a28-5c11-99ed-a06a6384cb26" :answer/id #uuid "0c3ac195-5a28-5c11-99ed-a06a6384cb26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{12\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-333420d8-3841-5986-a880-12239e3b0c86" :answer/id #uuid "333420d8-3841-5986-a880-12239e3b0c86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" :answer/id #uuid "8f4dc2f2-4b7b-5c53-9837-433249177feb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-867968e5-1470-5d6b-bbb2-c22bb39822ef" :answer/id #uuid "867968e5-1470-5d6b-bbb2-c22bb39822ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-206381c1-a55a-5bbf-8129-7ade3cea9753" :answer/id #uuid "206381c1-a55a-5bbf-8129-7ade3cea9753" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi }{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130466 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}, \\qquad\nt \\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nIf we rotate this curve by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we obtain a sphere of radius $1.$ \n\n![](images/d0/d0d323b764b70d3f2830d821636a9bc27b40e7f7e9f45df909c81dd279dc16e4.png)\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area of our surface of revolution can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-2\\pi (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-2\\pi (-1-1) \\\\  & =4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129340 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
